  "title": "In the Kitchen: Ugali and Sukuma",
  "category": "food-drink",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "level": 2,
  "order": 202,
  "theme": "kitchen",
  "culturalNote": "Ugali — stiff maize porridge eaten with your right hand — is Kenya's national dish, and sukuma wiki (collard greens, lit. \"push the week\") is its faithful partner because it's cheap enough to stretch the money to the end of the week. Making ugali has its own verb: kusonga, to stir and press the flour into the boiling water with a wooden mwiko. Kenyans say \"ninasikia njaa\" — lit. \"I hear hunger\" — for \"I'm hungry\".",
  "startingPoint": "Your neighbour Mama Njeri has invited you into her kitchen to learn the most important Kenyan recipe.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "infinitive-ku",
    "present-na",
    "adjective-agreement"
